    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. So you're saying Marcus and Andre fit the crime genre better because of their background/environment, while the others fit the political thriller because of their professions? \n\nI'm just wondering if that's a bit of a shortcut. Like, could Marcus or Andre be the ones in the high-stakes political roles too, or does their 'neighborhood' context make them feel less 'believable' in those high-power circles? Do you think audiences would find it weird if I swapped them around?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is not a hard rule that background dictates genre; modern thrillers frequently mix these elements. Marcus or Andre could absolutely function in high-power circles if their character arc demands a collision between their street intelligence and the political arena. Audiences generally respond well to when characters bring a grounded perspective into an elite setting, as it raises the stakes. The key is to write the transition convincingly, showing *why* they are in that high-stakes environment.",
      "source": "target",
